#dictionary is a collection of key value pairs
#dictionaries are defined using curly braces {} and key value pairs are separated by a colon :
#dictionaries are mutable, which means you can change the values of existing keys or add new key value pairs to the dictionary after it has been created.
#dictionaries are unordered, which means that the order of the key value pairs is not guaranteed
#dictionaries are indexed by keys, which means you can access the values of a dictionary using their corresponding keys.    
#dictionaries are commonly used to store and retrieve data based on unique keys, such as names, IDs, or any other identifier.
#creating a dictionary  
dict1 = {"name": "John", "age": 30, "city": "New York"} # data of string, int, string
print(dict1)
print(dict1["name"]) # accessing the value of a key
print(dict1["age"]) # accessing the value of a key
print(dict1["city"]) # accessing the value of a key
print(dict1.keys())
print(dict1.values())
print(dict1.items())
#modifying a dictionary
dict1["age"] = 31 # changing the value of an existing key
dict1["country"] = "USA" # adding a new key value pair to the dictionary    
print(dict1)


